Dear 3DPrinting.com team,

your 2026 software guide separates full CAD, mesh editing, repair and slicing.
Solidon3D 0.3.1 may be a useful addition in the gap between those categories:
it adapts a dimensionally wrong but otherwise usable downloaded model without
requiring the reader to rebuild it in a full CAD system.

A short review test would be straightforward. Import an STL whose mounting
holes are in the wrong place, click one, and move it. Solidon keeps the
original axis, stores the edit as a reversible operation, and — unlike the
usual fill-and-redrill workaround — keeps the feature's identity, so anything
referring to it still does.

The features most relevant to your guide are:

* recognised holes, pegs, countersinks and domes in imported STL and 3MF can be
  moved, rotated, resized, duplicated and removed;
* a feature panel shows the measured values as editable fields with a live
  preview, and one option applies an edit to all features of the same kind;
* printability checks and fit values based on a user-printed calibration part;
* per-spool process settings carried through 3MF to the slicer, with the
  printer profiles filtered to the configured machine;
* Windows, macOS and Linux support, with no account or cloud project storage.

Solidon is neither a general-purpose CAD replacement nor a slicer. Its purpose
is the practical step from "downloaded model" to "part that fits."

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Changelog: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

I can provide the released 0.3.1 build, a deliberately flawed STL and exact
before-and-after measurements if you would like to evaluate it for the guide
or a separate review.

